{"translation": "[11.1.2] [2. On fat and suet.] Fat (pimelē) and suet (stear). These too are among the commonest of the parts in animals. For all well-nourished animals have fat or suet, whereas lean and ill-nourished ones often appear to have none at all, and often have so little, and that so dry, that it is hard to use. Suet differs from fat in thickness; for this reason suet arises in animals whose whole nature is more earthy, just as fat does in those whose nature is more moist. For this very reason, then, fat melts easily under fire, but once melted does not easily congeal; suet, by contrast, does not melt easily, and if it is melted it congeals very quickly and appears much harder than fat. The pig, then, when well nourished, has a very great deal of fat on account of the natural moistness of its mixture, whereas oxen and goats and all other horned animals produce suet in greatest quantity on account of their dryness. You may, if you wish, call the whole oleaginous and oily substance in animals suet, as most physicians do; and you may also name this whole kind fat. You may not, however, if you wish not to speak falsely, say that the suet of goats is moister than that of pigs. For the latter is the moistest of nearly all the others, and for this reason in its action it is close to oil in power, though it is more softening than oil and more concoctive. And for this reason it is mixed into the poultices suited to inflammations. But for those who are bitten in the region of the rectum or the colon we inject goat's rather than pig's suet, not because it is more blunting of pungency (for, so far as its nature goes, pig's suet blunts more, and for this reason it is also mixed into drugs that treat ulcers, such as the one called parygron), but because goat's suet congeals easily on account of its thickness, whereas pig's flows away just like oil; for this reason we inject the former rather in dysenteric and tenesmic patients, when we wish to soothe their biting. Some things also blunt pungencies more on account of fineness of particles, when what is giving pain lies in the depth of the bodies that are being bitten. For the coarse-particled passes less, and the fine-particled more, through whole bodies, and mixes itself with all the biting ichors. In this respect, at any rate, goose suet blunts more effectively what bites in the depth of bodies. It is also hotter than pig's. Between them lies that of cocks; and that of male animals is always hotter and drier. Among these themselves, that of those whose testicles are excised is less hot and dry; for the castrated male always comes to resemble the females of the same kind. The general heading of the account, which must be remembered, is this: that the differences of the fat or suet in animals (or however you wish to name this whole kind of oleaginous and oily body in animals) always arise according to the mixture of the animals. Since, then, the pig falls short in heat and dryness of nearly all four-footed animals, for this reason it also has fat that is both less hot and more moist. Now the power of all fat is moistening and heating of human bodies; for this must be remembered throughout, having been stated at the beginning of this treatise; and the differences in each of them are matters of more and less. For the fat of the pig, relative to us, has moistening in abundance, but heating not in the same way as oil; rather, the fat (lipos) of pigs is on a level with the heat in us. That of bulls is much hotter and drier than that of pigs, we again remembering here too that the male is always drier and hotter than the female, and that the castrated male resembles the female, as indeed does everything young; and that among the young themselves the female is moister and less hot than the male. Thus calf's suet falls short of that of bulls in heat and dryness, and that of kids of that of goats, and that of goats falls short of that of he-goats, and again that of bulls in turn falls short of that of lions; for that is the most dispersing of nearly all those in quadrupeds. For it is also the hottest of them and the most fine-particled, so that if you mix it into the drugs suited to ulcers and inflammations, you will not only do no good but will even do harm, having made the drug more pungent than is fitting. But for chronic hardened swellings, contractures (ankylai) of sinews, and in short whatever is scirrhous, it is most suitable, in cases in which that of pigs appears most ineffective. That of bulls stands roughly equally distant from each, being as much hotter and drier than that of pigs as it falls short of that of lions. As being placed in the middle, then, it is reasonably mixed into each kind of drug, both those that treat scirrhous conditions and those that concoct inflamed parts, such as the one called tetrapharmakon, composed of wax, resin, pitch and suet. For into this, whether you put bull's or calf's or he-goat's or goat's or pig's, you will make a suppurative and concoctive drug. But pig's, when put in, suits children and women and, in a word, the soft-fleshed, while bull's is useful for diggers, reapers and all who have hard flesh, whether through their natural mixture or through the kind of life they lead. All suet, as it ages, becomes hotter and more fine-particled than it was, and so also more dispersing. This holds for nearly all things that age, at least those that do not rot first. For wine, honey, vinegar, cheese, butter and oesypum, and every oil, whether mastic oil or castor oil or radish oil or the very oil from olives, become thinner and more fine-particled as they age, and for this reason appear more pungent to those who taste them and more dispersing to those who use them, in all hardened and hard-to-resolve affections. I, then, have said that they appear more pungent to those who taste them. But some of those who corrupt the meanings of words call all such things not pungent but astringent, even down to pepper, as if it made no difference whether one says astringent or pungent. And if you ask them in turn about oak-galls, myrtles, medlars, pomegranate rinds, omphakion and sumach, they say that these too are astringent, although the perception we have from them is the most opposite to that which arises in us from pepper, pyrethron, mustard, euphorbium, onion, garlic and adarce. If, then, just as they apply the name of astringency in common to both of the affections arising in us in taste, so too they think that the affections themselves have a single nature, they must be considered anything rather than human beings, as alone possessing senses peculiar to themselves. But if they agree that unripe grape, sumach, oak-gall, balaustion and hypocistis draw together and constrict our substance, while pepper, mustard and pyrethron separate, bite and pass through it, then they improperly name the biting and heating things astringent, but they have not erred in their knowledge of the drugs themselves. If one must say by conjecture whether they err in the names or in the things, I for my part would say that these men have erred rather in the names, and especially those unaccustomed to the Greek tongue, such as Dioscorides of Anazarbus, who has said many things well among the matters recorded concerning medical material, but is ignorant of the meanings of Greek words. When this man, then, says that the suet of goats is more astringent than that of pigs, if by 'more astringent' he wishes to indicate 'more pungent', we shall accept the statement as true; but if he means what has the kind of quality and power that sumach and rhēon, hypocistis and balaustion have, we shall say that the statement is not true. Since they also say that the suet of vipers, when someone plucks out the hairs in the armpits by the roots and anoints the place, prevents these from growing again, I thought it better to test this too, and having done as they bade I found them to be lying, as also in the claim that it cures the beginnings of hypochyses. That the suet of bears suits alopecias they wrote truly, but we have more approved drugs than it for the affection. As for that of foxes, however, when they say that it treats earaches without distinguishing what sort of earaches they mean, as men who do not even know articulately what they are saying, one should not pay any attention to them at all. Others praise the suet of fishes, as being for hypochyses or whatever affection they wish to name. Others again write not simply of fishes, but of river fishes, and others rather of sea fishes, so that they may seem to know something more than the many.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 350, "surface": "ὑείου.", "english": "pig's", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 469, "surface": "θερμαντικὴ", "english": "heating", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 852, "surface": "ῥαφάνινον", "english": "radish oil", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 929, "surface": "μεσπίλων", "english": "medlars", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 964, "surface": "ἀδάρκης.", "english": "adarce", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 1014, "surface": "βαλαύστιόν", "english": "balaustion", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 1035, "surface": "νᾶπυ", "english": "mustard", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 1037, "surface": "πύρεθρον,", "english": "pyrethron", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ἀλλ’ ὅτι (probably corrupt for ἄλλο τι, rendered 'anything rather than')", "ἐκπυπτικόν (taken as ἐκπυητικόν, 'suppurative')", "ῥῆον (rhēon; possibly a corruption, or rhubarb)", "ὀμφακίου (omphakion, unripe-grape juice)", "πάρυγρον (name of a drug)", "οἴσυπος (oesypum, wool-grease)"]}
