{"translation": "[10.2.2] [2. On pig's blood.] Pig's blood, then, is moist and less hot, and most closely resembles human blood in its mixture, since the flesh of pigs too resembles that of humans. Indeed, we already know of many innkeepers and cooks who were caught selling human flesh as pork, and yet none of those who ate it perceived any difference. Moreover, I heard trustworthy people relate that in a certain inn they had eaten abundant broth with most delicious meat, and when they had already eaten their fill, they found in it the front part of a finger, at the nail itself. Fearing that the people in the inn might eat them too, as being accustomed to the practice, they left at once, vomited up what they had eaten, and continued their journey. They also said that not long afterwards the people at the inn were caught for the human beings they used to slaughter. So one might reasonably suppose that the blood of pigs too is most similar to that of humans. For it is from such blood that the flesh has both its first generation and its nourishment thereafter. Hence whoever says that human blood is useful against some disease, let him first demonstrate in pig's blood, if not the same action, at least a similar one. For even if it should fall short of human blood in some respect of its power, it will at any rate do something comparable, and once this is known we shall expect the benefit from human blood to be more complete. But if it appears to preserve not even a little of what is promised, there is no reason to make trial of human blood either. For we have found by experience that similar things are productive of similar effects. At any rate, among us in Pergamum, and indeed throughout almost the whole of Asia, those who have trepanned fractured bones of the head are accustomed to pour dove's blood onto the thick membrane [κατασχεῖν, probably to be read καταχεῖν]. Then someone who happened to have a wood-pigeon but not a dove used the wood-pigeon's blood, and the man was nevertheless saved. And another, using the blood of a turtle-dove on another patient, did the man no harm. For to use similar things for the same affections when those tested beforehand are not available is one of the things agreed upon and attested by experience. And indeed most of human life is carried on in this way.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 2, "surface": "ὑείου", "english": "pig's", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 4, "surface": "Ὕειον", "english": "pig's", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 66, "surface": "ζωμὸν", "english": "broth", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 67, "surface": "δαψιλῆ", "english": "abundant", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 107, "surface": "ὁδοιπορίας", "english": "journey", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 148, "surface": "γένεσίς", "english": "generation", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 189, "surface": "ἀνθρωπείου", "english": "human", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 230, "surface": "περιστερᾶς", "english": "dove's", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["κατασχεῖν", "κατ’ αὐτὸν τὸν ὄνυχα", "ἐφ’ οἷς ἔσφαττον ἀνθρώπους"]}
