{"translation": "[10.2.13] [13. On bile.] This humour has been shown to be the hottest of the humours in each animal. Just as animals have blood and flesh that are unlike in their mixture, so by the same reasoning they also have unlike bile. For in the hottest animals the bile too must necessarily exceed the biles of the other animals, while in those less hot than these this humour too falls short in proportion, by as much as it falls short in the other things as well. For you have learned that all the humours in each of the blooded animals are four: blood, phlegm, and yellow and black bile. You have also learned that it is the custom not only of physicians but of all the Greeks to call yellow bile simply 'bile', on the understanding that the hearers will supply the name of the colour, but not to call black bile simply 'bile'; instead, with this whole addition, they call it 'black bile'. You also know that they sometimes call yellow bile 'pale yellow', and that in the bladder on the liver we see it in the dissections of animals sometimes pale yellow and sometimes yellow. You have also heard that yellow bile is hotter than pale yellow bile by as much as it is also thicker. For when a serous moisture is mixed with yellow bile, the pale yellow comes to be, just as the pale yellow, when heated further, is rendered thick in consistency and yellow in colour. And indeed in animals too it appears yellow in those that are hotter and pale yellow in those that are less so. And whenever hot animals happen to have gone hungry and thirsty, it turns towards the dark colour: sometimes this is verdigris-like, sometimes dark blue, and sometimes that of woad, which is duskier than that of cabbage. Pay attention, then, you too, to the colour of the biles whenever you prepare a drug in which some bile is also contained. Into the so-called kykliskoi and trochiskoi, such as that of Andron, of Polyeides, of Pasion and that of Bitinus, they mix the bile of the bull. When they prepare drugs useful for the eyes, they use that of the hyena, the cock, the partridge and certain other animals. The bull's bile is evidently hotter and more drying than that of castrated oxen. For the castrated animal always comes to resemble the female and the young. Just as these exceed the mature in moisture but fall short in heat, in the same way the castrated ox falls short of bulls. And I have seen the bile of some bulls dark blue, the yellow having been over-roasted; I did not think fit to put it into the drug being prepared, but chose the moderately yellow bile of another bull. For that bull had been exhausted by excessive toil, being dragged with violent bonds, so that it is quite clear that, having also been enraged in this work, it had a hotter mixture. It was likely too that it had been thirsty and hungry while being dragged violently. To speak in summary: just as urine appears moderately pale yellow with moderate drinking, yellow in those who have been thirsty, have gone without food and have toiled much, and white in those who have been filled and become drunk, so bile too changes its colours, towards the yellower in exercises, fasting and thirst, and towards the whiter in the opposite conditions. Know then that if you put markedly yellow bile into the drug being prepared, you will make it hotter; if pale yellow bile, moderately hot; and if bile that is slack and watery, falling short of the appropriate mixture by as much as the bile has turned towards the whiter. In this way, then, for those who open closed haemorrhoids by means of bull's bile, it has sometimes appeared weak and sometimes pungent beyond what is appropriate. Some difference also arises from the body that uses the bile, according as it is sensitive or insensitive, readily affected or not readily affected. But from the bile itself, as I said, the variation is not small. At any rate the same person is bitten more by yellow bile and less by pale yellow. It is possible, then, to see clearly how pungent this humour is from its opening haemorrhoids; but it also appears biting to those who use it, and for this reason we take care not to use yellow bile by itself alone for any other disease or symptom. For the kykliskoi mentioned take little of the bile; and of the drugs compounded for sharpness of sight, some take hyena's bile, others that of the partridge or the cock or some other animal, and they contain honey mixed in and fennel juice or opobalsam. The bile of certain animals has been especially praised by physicians as at once sharpening the sight and dispersing the beginnings of suffusions, such as that of the fish they call kallionymos, and that of the hyena, of the sea scorpion, of the hen and of the partridge. Weakest is that of swine, to such a degree that it is not even intolerable to ulcers; it appears the most watery of all biles, except that of wild swine that live in the mountains. For just as their whole flesh is hotter and drier, so too is their bile. Some use the bile of domestic swine for ulcers in the ears, a drug not without repute, and you too should use it when no other of the compound drugs is available, for there are countless of them. According to the magnitude of the condition, the bile of some other animal could also be suitable. For when the ulcer is chronic and has much ichor and pus, it will tolerate the more drying biles as well, such as that of sheep, which is slightly more pungent than that of swine, and, more so than this, that of goats, to which that of bears and of oxen is somewhat similar. That of bulls is stronger than these, but falls short of that of hyenas. This in turn falls short of that of the kallionymos, the sea scorpion and the sea turtle. Some have written that the bile of the wild goat benefits those suffering from nyctalopia. The biles of winged animals are all more pungent and more drying than those in quadrupeds, and among the winged animals themselves those of hens and partridges are better for medical use. Those of hawks and eagles are markedly pungent and are also erosive; for this reason they also appear verdigris-like in colour, and sometimes even black. Knowing, then, these differences among them, and knowing also which affections need to be dried more and which less, if you have tested any one bile whatsoever by its effects, you will be able to pass from these to the others by method, so as always to employ the one that suits the affection. For since the intensity of each power lies in conjecture, you must pass from some defined starting-point to the untested material, having learned the degrees of excess among them from what has been tested beforehand. And I have also gone through for you clear signs taken from the colour of the biles and the mixture of the animals.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 116, "surface": "χρόας", "english": "colour", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 308, "surface": "ὑαίνης", "english": "hyena", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 651, "surface": "μαράθρου", "english": "fennel", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 655, "surface": "ἐνίων", "english": "certain", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 685, "surface": "θαλαττίου", "english": "sea", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 785, "surface": "ἰχῶρά", "english": "ichor", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 885, "surface": "ἀλεκτορίδων", "english": "hens", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 902, "surface": "δριμεῖαι", "english": "pungent", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["Βιτῖνος", "ὑπεροπτηθείσης", "ἰῶδες", "φαιότερον", "ὑποχυμάτων", "ἔκλυτον"]}
