{"translation": "[10.2.19] [19. On dog's dung.] I myself, at any rate, know it, having experienced the wonderful power both of human dung and of dog's dung. I shall tell you first about the dog's, which one of our teachers used continually. He gave a dog nothing but bones to eat for two days in succession, as a result of which the dung becomes hard and white and least ill-smelling. Taking this, then, he dried it, so that later, whenever he wished to use it, it could easily be ground fine. He used it for synanchai, for dysenteries and for the most chronic of ulcers. For synanchai he mixed it with the drugs that are suited to this affection in any case. In the case of dysenteric patients he mixed it with boiled milk into which the so-called pebbles (kachlēkes), red-hot, had been thrown, or, as I later came to do with iron because it is easy to procure, throwing this red-hot into milk that had first been well boiled. Some of the physicians, I do not know why, call such milk 'split' (schiston). For this ought not to be called so; rather, the name belongs to the cases in which we separate and split the whey from the cheesy part. But the physician ground some dry dog's dung and threw it, without its being noticed, into the milk that had the pebbles quenched in it, and he taught this to his most genuine pupils alone. Of these two uses of dog's dung, then, I have a very great deal of experience, as of a wonderful drug, just as I have of its use for the most malignant ulcers. For in those cases too he mixed a little dog's dung with the approved drugs, and the drug clearly became stronger than it had been by itself. And wherever there was need to disperse and dry something, he mixed it into those drugs as well.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 2, "surface": "κυνείας", "english": "dog's", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 46, "surface": "δυσώδης", "english": "ill-smelling", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 68, "surface": "δυσεντερίας", "english": "dysenteries", "uncertain": "n"},