{"translation": "[9.2.3] [3. On schistos and many other stones.] A power similar to that of this stone, but weaker, is possessed by the stone called schistos, and after it by galaktites. Melitites, as I said, has already taken on something of heat as well. Each of them, then, departing little by little from the power of haematites, is likewise brought into use for the eyes, but is milder. Milder drugs are always gentler for parts that are still inflamed, but weaker for bringing to complete health parts that have ceased to be inflamed. Drying is common to all stones. Those of them which, whether dissolved into a juice or pounded fine in some other way, have at first no quality perceptible to the taste must be considered the weakest, the gentlest and the least biting. Those which appear to display some clear quality to the taste have a strength proportionate to that quality. If they are astringent, their strength is for repressing, constricting, compacting and drawing together. If they are biting, it is for heating, dispersing and melting. Intermediate between them are those that are only cleansing, without being astringent or biting. Now haematites and schistos, galaktites and melitites dissolve into a juice when rubbed, as I said, on whetstones and in mortars. There are also other stones that dissolve into a juice, such as the one produced in Egypt, which people use to glaze linen cloths. This stone is more devoid of quality than those already mentioned, displaying neither astringency nor cleansing nor biting. Hence this very thing alone belongs to this stone, namely drying. For this reason they mix it with cerate and use it for the cicatrization of ulcers in soft bodies. Such stones are also mixed into ophthalmic preparations, in the same way as those mentioned before. By as much as it is more softening than they are, through having no active quality, by so much it is more moderate and produces less sensation. Some people call this stone moroxos, others graphis.", 