{"translation": "[9.3.14] [14. On lye made from ash.] Lye. Lye comes into being when ash has been washed; and the Greeks call 'ash' the residue of burnt bodies, whether these be stony in nature or parts of trees or of animals. The lye, then, also comes to be according to the nature of what is washed: from that which has a most pungent fiery quality acquired in burning, it is most pungent; from that which is not of this kind, it is more moderate. For this reason it is also mixed into the so-called putrefying preparations, since it has a caustic heat, burning painlessly through the fine-particled character of its substance.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 2, "surface": "κονίας", "english": "lye", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 6, "surface": "τέφρας.]", "english": "ash", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 9, "surface": "τέφρας", "english": "ash", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 17, "surface": "ὑπόλειμμα", "english": "residue", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 31, "surface": "δένδρων", "english": "trees", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 48, "surface": "ἐμπύρευμα", "english": "fiery quality acquired in burning", "uncertain": "y"}, {"target_no": 56, "surface": "μετριωτέρα,", "english": "more moderate", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 64, "surface": "καυστικὴν", "english": "caustic", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ἐμπύρευμα", "δυνάμεσι"]}
