{"translation": "[6.1.1] [1. On southernwood and wormwood and the species of each.] Of southernwood, this herb, one need not describe the form, after so many men of such quality, nor its particular actions, since those men set them out, if not with precise distinctions, at least clearly. More will also be said by us about them in the treatise on the composition of drugs and in that on easily procured remedies, and sometimes also in the writings of the Therapeutic Method, when need calls. Only what was proposed from the beginning, to examine the universal powers of all drugs, follows in the case of the others too, and it must now already be done here. It must be said that southernwood is hot and dry in power, ranked somewhere in the third order and remove beyond the balanced state, and has a certain dispersing and cutting power. Of the same power also is its rubbing (?) having taken [it] (τρίψις … εἰληφυῖα), as also the flesh-forming and the biting. That such an order is assessed relative to the well-tempered nature has often been said before. We found out its mixture not least by judging also from taste, for it is quite bitter. Such a flavour was shown to be earthy in its substance and to be thinned by abundant heat, so as both to heat and to dry not feebly. Moreover, having tested it accurately also by the qualified experience, about which it has often been said before, we found this drug to be of the same mixture. For if you pound the foliage together with the flowers (for the rest of it, the dry stalk, is useless) and sprinkle it on a clean ulcer, it appears biting and irritant; and if you steep it in oil and choose to pour it over either the head or the belly, it will be found to heat strongly. And indeed those who are seized by rigors at periods, if you rub them down with it before the onset, shiver less; nor does it escape perception, immediately as it is applied, that it heats. That it is likely to destroy worms, being bitter, is clear even before experience, if we remember anything of what was said in the fourth of these commentaries about the nature of the bitter flavour. You will know at once that it also has a certain dispersing and cutting power. And that this must belong to it more than to wormwood you will be able to infer, first from the taste. For southernwood partakes of a very small acerbity, but wormwood of not a little; and then also from the fact that southernwood is bad for the stomach, as is seriphon too, whereas wormwood is good for the stomach. For it was shown earlier about these things too that the bitter by itself is wholly bad for the stomach, whereas the harsh or acerbic or, in general, the astringent is good for the stomach; and when the qualities are mixed with one another, the stronger would prevail. This, then, is enough for you to know in this treatise. For it will be shown in the writings of the Therapeutic Method how one may best use such a drug. And for this reason do not seek any longer to hear either that, applied as a poultice with boiled quince or with bread, it heals inflammations of the eyes, or that, boiled smooth with ōmēlysis, it disperses phymata. For neither of these nor any of the others is proper to the present treatise; rather, for those who give an empirical teaching it is written in the easily procured drugs, while those who wish to practise the art rationally have need of the Therapeutic Method. For, among other things, one would be harmed rather than benefited by such a report. Now Hippocrates, writing in the Aphorisms, 'pains of the eyes are resolved by drinking of unmixed wine, or a bath, or a vapour-bath, or venesection, or purging', yet not adding what kind of pains drinking of unmixed wine resolves, what kind a bath, which a vapour-bath, which venesection, which purging, one would pardon, I think, for three reasons. For he was giving an aphoristic teaching, in which, because of brevity, many things are allowed to be stated in this way; and he wrote down all the remedies of the pains, even if he did not distinguish which of them suits which kind of pain; or else in many places of his other writings he has given us starting-points for the distinctions in what was stated in this way. But those who have written about such aphorisms neither in other books nor in a detailed and long treatise, but write aphoristically and briefly, and besides this (εἴτε τὸ πρὸς τούτοις) indicate one thing out of many, harm us in more cases than they help. For since there are many differentiae among ophthalmias, and one of them requires the aforementioned poultice while the others are harmed by it, he who uses it on all of them without distinction will harm many more than he helps. In this manner, then, we must write not about southernwood alone but about all the others as well, the powers relating to heating and cooling or moistening or drying being found (εὑρίσκουσιν) by the methods I have often spoken of, but whatever is effected by the peculiarity of the whole substance by experience alone. It has been shown about such things too that they are deleterious, and protective against deleterious things, and purgative. For of these it is not possible to make a rational discovery; rather, it is only possible to find in some cases a certain plausible conjecture, and not indeed in all, just as this very point has been made clear in what precedes. But about the powers found in this way I shall give an account separately in what follows, once I have first gone through, for each species of drug, what concerns heating and cooling, moistening and drying, and whatever follows on these. Having added this much more about southernwood, however, I shall end the discussion: that the most admirable Pamphilus, although writing of this as his first herb, and having perhaps wished, if of none of the following, at least of this one to become an eyewitness, nevertheless erred most greatly, believing that the plant is called santonicum by the Romans. For southernwood differs